# What are the pros and cons of year-round schooling?

I've heard of year-round schooling in many places, but I've never been to a school that uses it. How is it done, and what are the advantages and disadvantages?

I don't know of any schools in the UK that do it, but I've seen it suggested in the USA.  I'm not sure what the advantages would be; it seems to me that if you are going to school in the summer, then you're not going to get a summer job, and if you're going to get a summer job, then you're not going to be going to school in the summer.  I suppose it could be done with longer holidays, but then you lose the advantage of not being on holiday for 2 months at a time.

-
@Matt: In the US, many school districts have summer school for kids who are behind in their studies or who have failed a class. I'm not sure how that would work if the school was year-round. – Ben Alpert Jan 14 '10 at 0:59
@Ben: I don't see why it couldn't work, the classes would just be smaller and probably more intense. – Qiaochu Yuan Jan 14 '10 at 1:01
@Qiaochu: Sure, but if they're smaller, it may not be cost-effective to have the school open. I've heard of some districts that don't have school on Fridays, for example, to save on costs. – Ben Alpert Jan 14 '10 at 1:04
@Ben: Ah, that's a good point. – Qiaochu Yuan Jan 14 '10 at 1:05
@Ben: I'm not sure about the UK, but here in Australia summer school is usually for students who are failing, or who want to finish high school a year early. – Nick Jan 14 '10 at 1:06

